#- Shapes
#-
#- A shape groups values by giving names to the tapes of a run. It is not a new
#- kind of value: Point{10, 20} is two tapes laid end to end, and nothing in it
#- says a shape built it. There is no header, no length and no tag.
#-
#- So `shape` and `as` are directives for whoever writes the source. They let
#- the compiler turn a name into an index, point at a mistake where it was
#- written, and tell the editor what is there — and then they are gone. Nothing
#- about them reaches the compiled program.
#-
#- Run: aurora run examples/shapes.ar
#-
#- Output:
#-   10
#-   20
#-   10 20
#-   [0 0 0 0 0 0 0 10 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 20]
#-   200
#-   14
#-   1
#-   24930 98
#-   0
#-   0
#-   5
#-   1

shape Point { x, y };

ident p = Point{10, 20};

#- A field reads one tape of the run, by the index its name was declared at.
printd p.x;
printd p.y;

#- The value itself is the run, and printd walks tapes without being told
#- anything about shapes.
printd p;
printb p;

#- Behaviour lives in defer, and a defer receives values through feed. The shape
#- does not survive that crossing — nothing in the bytes carries it — so it is
#- named again with "as". The annotation is a claim, not a check.
ident area = defer {
  ident q = feed(0) as Point;
  q.x * q.y;
};

printd area(Point{10, 20});

#- "as" is an expression, so it does not need a name to hang on.
ident twice = defer { (feed(0) as Point).y * 2; };
printd twice(Point{3, 7});

#- Two shapes of the same width are the same value. There is nothing in a run
#- of tapes that says which shape built it.
shape Pair { a, b };
printd Point{1, 2} equals Pair{1, 2};

#- A field is a tape, and text is a tape, so text goes in a field like anything
#- else — as much of it as a tape holds.
printd Pair{"ab", 98};

#- Reading past the end gives the neutral value instead of stopping the program,
#- the way head saturates and feed wraps. A claim can be wrong.
ident single = 7 as Point;
printd single.y;


#- "as" is what whoever reads a value claims about it. "returns" is what a block
#- promises about itself, and the compiler refuses the block that does not keep
#- it — so a scope that says it answers with a Result and ends with a number
#- does not compile.
#-
#- What it buys is the other end: a call to a scope that promised has a shape,
#- so nobody has to claim one at every call site.
shape Result { failed, value };

ident divide = defer {
  if feed(1) equals 0 {
    Result{1, 0};
  } else {
    Result{0, feed(0) / feed(1)};
  };
} returns Result;

ident r = divide(10, 2);
printd r.failed;
printd r.value;

#- And it reads straight off the call, since the shape came with it.
printd divide(1, 0).failed;
